The Kalachakra, or Wheel of Time, is the most elaborate mandala in the Buddhist
world. Its name points to its subject: it is a diagram of time, mapping cycles
of breath, days, and cosmic ages onto a single layered palace.

## A palace seen from above

Like other Tibetan mandalas, the Kalachakra is read as architecture, a
multi-storied palace viewed from directly overhead, with gates at the four
directions and a deity at the center.

## A cosmos and a clock

What sets it apart is its scope. The Kalachakra correlates the outer cosmos,
the inner body, and the cycles of time, treating them as reflections of one
another.

